.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
.Dd October 12, 2026
.Dt DTFL_COLLECTION_JOIN 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm dtfl_collection_join ,
.Nm dtfl_collection_join_map ,
.Nm dtfl_collection_semijoin ,
.Nm dtfl_collection_antijoin ,
.Nm dtfl_arranged_join_core ,
.Nm dtfl_join_emit_record
.Nd match pairs of records by key
.Sh LIBRARY
Timely and differential dataflow library
.Pq libdtfl , \-ldtfl
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In dtfl/arrange.h
.Ft void
.Fn dtfl_join_emit_record "dtfl_join_emit *e" "const void *rec" "size_t len"
.Ft dtfl_collection *
.Fn dtfl_arranged_join_core "dtfl_arranged *a1" "dtfl_arranged *a2" "const dtfl_type *out_type" "dtfl_join_result_fn result" "dtfl_ctx ctx"
.Ft dtfl_collection *
.Fn dtfl_collection_join_map "dtfl_collection *c1" "dtfl_collection *c2" "const dtfl_type *key_type" "const dtfl_type *val1_type" "const dtfl_type *val2_type" "const dtfl_type *out_type" "dtfl_join_result_fn result" "dtfl_ctx ctx"
.Ft dtfl_collection *
.Fn dtfl_collection_join "dtfl_collection *c1" "dtfl_collection *c2" "const dtfl_type *key_type" "const dtfl_type *val1_type" "const dtfl_type *val2_type" "const dtfl_type *out_type"
.Ft dtfl_collection *
.Fn dtfl_collection_semijoin "dtfl_collection *c" "dtfl_collection *keys" "const dtfl_type *key_type" "const dtfl_type *val_type" "const dtfl_type *out_type"
.Ft dtfl_collection *
.Fn dtfl_collection_antijoin "dtfl_collection *c" "dtfl_collection *keys" "const dtfl_type *key_type" "const dtfl_type *val_type" "const dtfl_type *out_type"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The join operators match records of two collections that share a key, as the
relational equijoin does.
A collection joined by key stores
.Pq key , value
records laid out as the key bytes followed by the value bytes; the key type
splits the record.
For each key present in both inputs, every value on one side is matched with
every value on the other, the matched diffs are multiplied, and the matched
times are joined.
The output is consolidated, so each
.Pq record , time
pair carries one net difference.
.Pp
.Fn dtfl_collection_join
matches
.Pq key , val1
in
.Va c1
with
.Pq key , val2
in
.Va c2
and yields the concatenation key||val1||val2.
.Va key_type ,
.Va val1_type
and
.Va val2_type
describe the shared key and the two values;
.Va out_type
describes the concatenated output record.
.Pp
.Fn dtfl_collection_join_map
is
.Fn dtfl_collection_join
with a result closure: for each matched
.Pq key , val1 , val2
the
.Va result
function emits zero or more output records of
.Va out_type
through the sink with
.Fn dtfl_join_emit_record ,
and the operator attaches the joined time and the product diff to each.
.Pp
.Fn dtfl_collection_semijoin
matches
.Pq key , val
in
.Va c
against the bare key records of
.Va keys ,
keeping the matched records of
.Va c
with multiplicities multiplied.
When
.Va keys
carries multiplicities of zero or one this is the relational semijoin;
otherwise it scales the counts.
.Va keys
is arranged by self, keying each record by itself.
.Pp
.Fn dtfl_collection_antijoin
subtracts the semijoin with
.Va keys
from
.Va c ,
discarding the records of
.Va c
whose key is present in
.Va keys .
The difference class of
.Va c
must be Abelian, since the operator negates the semijoin.
In almost all cases
.Va keys
should carry multiplicities of zero or one.
.Pp
.Fn dtfl_arranged_join_core
joins two arrangements that share a key type, the layer the collection
methods are built on.
It avoids re-indexing when the inputs are already arranged.
The two difference classes must have a declared product: an integer class
times the same integer class, the present class times any class, or a tuple
of integer classes times that integer.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
Each function returns a new
.Vt dtfl_collection
the caller owns and frees with
.Fn dtfl_collection_free .
.Fn dtfl_arranged_join_core ,
and the collection methods that reach it, return
.Dv NULL
and report
.Dv DTFL_EALGEBRA
when the two difference classes have no declared product.
.Fn dtfl_arranged_join_core
leaves the caller owning
.Va a1
and
.Va a2 .
.Sh OWNERSHIP
A result closure's context is released by its destructor when the operator
is dropped.
The value and key bytes passed to a result closure are borrowed for the call
only.
.Sh THREAD SAFETY
A join reads two arrangements on the worker that built them; every handle
belongs to that worker and must not be used from another thread.
When the inputs are collections, the operator exchanges their updates by key
so matching keys meet on one worker.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr dtfl_collection_arrange 3 ,
.Xr dtfl_collection_new 3 ,
.Xr dtfl-differential 7
.Sh STANDARDS
Ported from differential-dataflow 0.25.1
.Pq Pa operators/join.rs , Pa operators/arrange/arrangement.rs , Pa collection.rs .
